review_comments.body:
Claude suggested the following improvement.

```
1. Grow the buffer only when the batch can actually be completed (RecordsIterator.java:140-151). Today the grow path runs whenever the first batch doesn't fit in the buffer and the slice isn't fully read. The buffer is then sized to firstBatchSize, even when that batch runs past the end of the slice. That has two costs:

It allocates for a batch that can never be completed.
A corrupt length field (firstBatchSize() parses with maxMessageSize = Integer.MAX_VALUE) can trigger a very large allocation, e.g. an OutOfMemoryError on the controller while it reads a snapshot. The capped read would then fill only a small part of that buffer.
The old code had the same problem, but it's easy to fix here. Compare against the bytes the slice can still supply; that also replaces the bytesRead >= size check:



Integer firstBatchSize = memoryRecords.firstBatchSize();
int bytesAvailable = buffer.remaining() + (fileRecords.sizeInBytes() - bytesRead);
if (firstBatchSize == null || firstBatchSize <= buffer.remaining() || firstBatchSize > bytesAvailable) {
    return memoryRecords;
}
...
```

